\details{
\strong{Working resolution is decided here, not by the caller.} A crown is 3 to
10 m across; segmenting a 0.20 m CHM neither adds silvicultural information
nor fits in memory — the Couchey CHM is 418 million cells. The CHM is
therefore aggregated to \code{resolution} (default 0.5 m) before anything else,
which divides the cost by 6 to 25, and further if the result would still
exceed \code{max_cells}.

The aggregation uses \strong{\code{max}, not \code{mean}}: the apexes are precisely what
local-maximum detection looks for, and a smoothing statistic would flatten
them away. This costs a slight upward bias on \code{h_max} (the tallest cell of
each aggregate wins) — deliberate, and preferable to losing a tree.

Heights are read back with a zonal statistic on the raster
(\code{\link[terra]{zonal}}), never with a global \code{values()} or \code{extract()}: the
same innocent-looking call cost a 3 h 20 pipeline run on 2026-08-22.

Crowns may overlap, and that is not a defect: the marking application keeps
the tallest, the one whose apex physically dominates the operator. No
gap-filling is attempted either — a position inside no crown writes nothing
rather than guessing the neighbouring tree.
